# Licence of the captured data

**Crawl permission: established, and unusually explicit.**
**Reuse permission: NOT established. Do not assert one.**

These are different questions and this file keeps them apart.

## Crawling

Checked 2026-09-24. `api.apify.com/robots.txt` is **13 bytes** -- `User-agent: *`
with no rules beneath it, so nothing is disallowed. Evaluated with
`webprobes/robots.py` on longest match, never a substring test.

`apify.com/robots.txt` goes further than silence:

    User-agent: *
    Content-Signal: search=yes, ai-input=yes, ai-train=yes
    Allow: /

That is an **affirmative grant** rather than the absence of a refusal, and it
names AI input and training specifically. Very few publishers in this fleet
state anything at all; this one opts in.

## Reuse and redistribution

**Unaddressed.** No licence field appears anywhere in the API payload -- unlike,
say, the Environment Agency, which states OGL v3 in `meta.licence` on every
response. Apify's terms of service have **not** been read for a redistribution
grant, so nothing here should be taken as one.

Permission to fetch is not permission to republish. Until someone reads the
terms and records what they say, treat the captured bytes as material this repo
holds, not material it licenses onward.

## Personal data

The store carries `username`, `userFullName` and `userPictureUrl` identifying the
developer who published each actor. `userFullName` is frequently a real personal
name. These are business counterparties publishing under their own handle rather
than private individuals, which is why the registry declares
`personal_data: parties_only` and not `none`.

The parser emits `username` and drops `userFullName` and `userPictureUrl`: the
handle is the joinable identifier, the display name and photograph are not
needed for any question this repo asks.
